{"backend": "tavily", "query": ""QVC" "gross leverage" "net leverage" "4.03x"", "results": [{"title": "QVC Is Failing a Leverage Test. That’s Not a Problem.", "url": "https://substack.com/home/post/p-161413457", "snippet": "Here’s how these two agreements differ. The Credit Agreement uses a net leverage test, and restricted payments are triggered at 4.0x debt-to-EBITDA. The Credit Agreement has a maximum Covenant Leverage Ratio of 4.5x. The Indentures use a gross leverage test, and restricted payments are triggered at 3.5x debt-to-EBITDA. The Indentures do not have a covenant leverage ratio, and the EBITDA definitions are not exactly the same either. [...] Under the Indentures, however, they have failed a leverage test. Their leverage test with the Indentures is a gross leverage test, so the cash at QVC, Inc. is not included in the calculation, making their ratio much higher—at 3.6x. Because of this, under the Indentures—not the Credit Agreement—QVC fails the 3.5x leverage ratio and has triggered “Restricted Payments.” [...] Credit Agreement leverage ratio would go down from 3.1x to 2.7x—close to their target. This still leaves parent-level cash at over $70 million. It’s worth noting that QVC has bought back shares before while having a 2.7x leverage ratio. I’m not saying they will, but it is clear they are in their best financial position in years. And barring any further setbacks, it seems likely that QVC will be at target leverage by the end of the year. I find it hard to believe their banks won’t extend their"}, {"title": "Fitch rates QVC senior secured note offering | Reuters", "url": "https://www.reuters.com/article/business/fitch-rates-qvc-senior-secured-note-offering-idUSWNA0105", "snippet": "at 2.3x and 4.4x respectively. While Liberty's leverage exceeds its 4x target, Fitch believes EBITDA growth at QVC over the next 12 months and upcoming Liberty maturities in 2013 will reduce Liberty's leverage to 4x. QVC's EBITDA grew 4% in 2011 and 7% in the first quarter of 2012. The ratings reflect Fitch's expectation that the company will continue to manage leverage on a Liberty consolidated basis. Over the long term, Fitch expects Liberty's gross unadjusted leverage to be managed within 4x [...] As of March 31, 2012, Fitch calculates QVC's unadjusted gross leverage (pro forma for the $500 million offering) at 1.7x and Liberty's unadjusted gross leverage at 3.8x. Liberty is expected to fund a portion of the initial cash balance for Liberty Ventures with borrowings under QVC's revolver; Fitch expects a QVC revolver balance of $1.3 billion to $1.5 billion. Assuming a $1.5 billion QVC revolver balance, Fitch calculates QVC and Liberty's unadjusted gross leverage at 2.3x and 4.4x [...] managed within 4x and QVC unadjusted gross leverage to be managed within 2.5x. Fitch's IDRs for Liberty and QVC reflect the consolidated legal entity/obligor credit profile, rather than the Liberty Interactive/Venture tracking stock structure. This view is primarily driven by Fitch's interpretation of the Liberty bond indentures. Fitch believes that the company could not spin out QVC without consent of the bondholders, based on the current asset mix at Liberty. QVC generates 86% and 95% of"}, {"title": "Scotts Miracle-Gro's Q1 Beat: A Tactical Reassessment of the Hawthorne Catalyst", "url": "https://www.ainvest.com/news/scotts-miracle-gro-q1-beat-tactical-reassessment-hawthorne-catalyst-2601", "snippet": "- Scotts Miracle-GroSMG-- reported Q1 fiscal 2026 net sales of $354.4M and EBITDA of $3M, exceeding expectations driven by early seasonal demand and margin improvements. - The Hawthorne divestiture triggered a $105M pre-tax impairment charge but reduced leverage to 4.03x net debt/EBITDA from 4.52x, enabling a $500M share repurchase program once leverage falls below 4.0x. - Management reaffirmed 2026 guidance with 32%+ adjusted gross margin targets and $275M free cash flow, focusing growth on lawn/garden while selling Hawthorne to Vireo Growth by Q2. For the first quarter of fiscal 2026, Scotts Miracle-GroSMG-- reported net sales of $354.4 million and non-GAAP adjusted EBITDA of $3 million, both ahead of expectations. It immediately improved the leverage ratio, bringing it down to 4.03x net debt to adjusted EBITDA from 4.52x the prior year. This provides a stable near-term baseline, with targets for low single-digit U.S. consumer sales growth, a non-GAAP adjusted gross margin of at least 32%, and free cash flow of $275 million. The thesis is clear: the Q1 beat is a positive sign for the core business, but the Hawthorne divestiture is the real catalyst, resetting the balance sheet and improving the financial profile. The Hawthorne sale is a clean, one-time financial reset. This is a non-cash hit that removes the business from the ongoing financials, but it also immediately improves the leverage ratio to 4.03x net debt to adjusted EBITDA. Management's immediate financial target is clear: a 3.0x to 3.5x leverage ratio is the stated "sweet spot." The current 4.03x is a step toward that goal, but it's still above the target range. The real near-term impact is the improved leverage and the focus on the core lawn and garden business, which management says is now the sole strategic focus. The key condition is that leverage must fall below 4.0x, which is a clear, measurable trigger tied to the financial reset from the Hawthorne sale. These initiatives are the vehicles to hit the long-term targets, but they must also deliver near-term margin expansion to support the buyback and the path to a 3.0x to 3.5x leverage ratio. The Hawthorne sale provides the financial runway."}, {"title": "Liberty Interactive and QVC Ratings Unaffected by HSN Acquisition", "url": "https://www.fitchratings.com/research/corporate-finance/liberty-interactive-qvc-ratings-unaffected-by-hsn-acquisition-06-07-2017", "snippet": "estimates QVC's gross leverage at 2.6x and Liberty's gross leverage at 3.4x as of March 31, 2017. Fitch continues to expect QVC will reduce total leverage to its 2.5x target within the next six to nine months. [...] Fitch believes the acquisition is neutral to Liberty's and QVC's ratings. Although the acquisition reduces Liberty's unadjusted gross leverage and improves the asset coverage of Liberty's unsecured debt, Fitch has always relied materially on QVC and viewed Liberty's other assets as providing incremental support. The 61.8% interest in HSN will be held by LIC and will not be included as part of QVC's legal/obligor structure. As a result, QVC's gross leverage will remain outside Fitch's target for [...] Fitch's target for the rating. Fitch would consider an upgrade if Liberty demonstrates sustained gross unadjusted leverage below 3.5x and QVC's unadjusted gross leverage is managed to below 2x."}, {"title": "What Is Gross Leverage and How Is It Calculated? - LegalClarity", "url": "https://legalclarity.org/what-is-gross-leverage-and-how-is-it-calculated", "snippet": "That 4.0x means the company carries four years’ worth of operating earnings in debt. If the company also holds $80 million in cash, a lender calculating net leverage would subtract that cash from the numerator: ($480 million − $80 million) ÷ $120 million = 3.3x. The gap between 4.0x and 3.3x illustrates exactly why the choice between gross and net matters so much in credit decisions.

## Gross Leverage vs. Net Leverage [...] Suppose a manufacturing company has $400 million in long-term debt, $50 million drawn on its revolving credit line, and $30 million in finance lease obligations. Its total debt is $480 million. The company’s TTM EBITDA is $120 million.

Gross leverage = $480 million ÷ $120 million = 4.0x [...] Gross leverage measures a company’s total debt relative to its earnings or assets, without subtracting any cash the company has on hand. The most common version divides total debt by EBITDA (earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortization), producing a multiple like 4.0x or 5.0x. That number tells you roughly how many years of operating earnings it would take to pay off the entire debt load. Lenders and credit analysts treat gross leverage as the conservative starting point for"}]}
